<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>introductory-microbiology</title></head><body><h1>introductory-microbiology</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Monera and Bacteria</li><li>Introductory Microbiology — Bacteria and Viruses</li><li>Monera: General introduction, structure of bacterial cell…</li><li>Impacts of Biotechnology in Microbiology</li><li>Virus: General introduction, structure and importance of …</li><li>Virus</li><li>Biotechnology in Microbiology</li></ul><h2>Monera and Bacteria</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Kingdom Monera: prokaryotic organisms (no membrane-bound nucleus); includes bacteria, cyanobacteria (blue-green algae), mycoplasma, and archaebacteria.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Bacterial cell: capsule (slime layer) → cell wall (peptidoglycan) → plasma membrane → cytoplasm; 70S ribosomes; naked circular DNA (nucleoid); may have pili, fimbriae, flagella, mesosomes.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Shapes: cocci (spherical), bacilli (rod), spirilla/spirochaetes (spiral). Arrangements: diplococci, streptococci, staphylococci.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Mode of nutrition: autotrophic (photoautotrophs, chemoautotrophs) and heterotrophic (saprophytes, parasites).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Bacterial growth: lag → log (exponential) → stationary → death phases; reproduction by binary fission (asexual) and by conjugation/transformation/transduction (genetic exchange).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Cyanobacteria: blue-green algae; photosynthetic; Nostoc, Anabaena (fix atmospheric nitrogen); important in rice paddies as biofertilisers.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Gram staining: Gram-positive (thick peptidoglycan, purple) vs Gram-negative (thin peptidoglycan + outer membrane, pink).</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Monera = prokaryotic: no true nucleus, 70S ribosomes, circular DNA.</td></tr><tr><td>Shapes: cocci, bacilli, spirilla; arrangements: diplo-, strepto-, staphylo-.</td></tr><tr><td>Nutrition: autotrophic (photo/chemo) + heterotrophic (saprophytes, parasites).</td></tr><tr><td>Growth curve: lag, log, stationary, death.</td></tr><tr><td>Binary fission = asexual reproduction; conjugation/transformation/transduction = genetic exchange.</td></tr><tr><td>Cyanobacteria: photosynthetic + nitrogen-fixing (Nostoc, Anabaena).</td></tr><tr><td>Gram stain: +ve (purple, thick wall) vs -ve (pink, thin wall + LPS).</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Bacteria are prokaryotic: they have no true nucleus and no membrane-bound organelles.</td></tr><tr><td>Cyanobacteria are bacteria (prokaryotes), NOT algae; they fix nitrogen but are not "true" algae.</td></tr><tr><td>Mycoplasma lacks a cell wall; it is the smallest known living cell.</td></tr><tr><td>Archaea differ from Bacteria in cell wall (no peptidoglycan) and membrane lipids; they live in extreme environments.</td></tr><tr><td>Binary fission produces two genetically identical daughter cells; conjugation transfers DNA but does not increase cell number.</td></tr><tr><td>Gram-positive bacteria have a THICK peptidoglycan layer; Gram-negative have a THIN layer plus an outer LPS membrane (more resistant to many antibiotics).</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Kingdom Monera comprises prokaryotic organisms: bacteria, cyanobacteria, mycoplasma, and archaebacteria. Bacteria show diverse shapes, nutrition, and reproduction (binary fission + genetic exchange). The growth curve (lag, log, stationary, death) and Gram staining are fundamental microbiology tools. Cyanobacteria fix nitrogen and photosynthesise. Bacteria are ecologically indispensable as decomposers, nitrogen fixers, and symbionts, and economically vital in food, industry, and medicine.</p><hr><h2>Introductory Microbiology — Bacteria and Viruses</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>DETAILED MONERA (bacterial + cyanobacterial) STRUCTURE AND PHYSIOLOGY.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Bacterial cell (Gram-negative example, E. coli): capsule (slime layer, polysaccharide, virulence) → cell wall (thin peptidoglycan + outer LPS membrane with porins) → plasma membrane (phospholipid + proteins, respiratory + metabolic enzymes) → cytoplasm (70S ribosomes, plasmids, nucleoid).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Bacterial cell (Gram-positive example, Staphylococcus): capsule → thick peptidoglycan cell wall (no outer membrane) → plasma membrane → cytoplasm; mesosomes (invaginations, respiration/secretion).</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Pili (fimbriae): short, many; attachment + conjugation (sex pilus); flagella: 1–many; locomotion (peritrichous, polar, lophotrichous).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Nucleoid: naked, circular, double-stranded DNA; no histones; 1–10 plasmids (small circular DNA, carry antibiotic-resistance + virulence genes).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Shapes: cocci (diplococci, streptococci, staphylococci, tetrads, sarcinae), bacilli (diplobacilli, streptobacilli), spirilla (spiral, rigid), spirochaetes (spiral, flexible).</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Nutrition: autotrophs (photoautotrophs — cyanobacteria; chemoautotrophs — nitrifiers) + heterotrophs (saprophytes, parasites).</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Growth curve: lag (adaptation) → log (exponential, 2ⁿ) → stationary (nutrient depletion + waste) → death (lysis); generation time (E. coli ~20 min).</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Reproduction: asexual (binary fission, budding in some) + genetic exchange (conjugation [F+ → F-], transformation [DNA uptake], transduction [phage-mediated]).</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Endospores: dormant, resistant structures (Bacillus, Clostridium); survive heat, radiation, chemicals; germinate when conditions improve.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Cyanobacteria: photosynthetic (chlorophyll a + phycobilins); Nostoc (beaded filaments), Anabaena (heterocysts for N2 fixation), Oscillatoria (trichoid); important in rice paddies as biofertilisers.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Mycoplasma: no cell wall; smallest living cell; pleomorphic; causes atypical pneumonia.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Archaebacteria: extreme environments (halophiles, thermoacidophiles, methanogens); no peptidoglycan; membrane lipids (ether linkages).</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>NEB focus: detailed bacterial cell (Gram + and -), shapes, nutrition, growth curve, reproduction, cyanobacteria, mycoplasma, and archaebacteria.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Bacteria are the simplest, minute, unicellular prokaryotes that can survive even in diverse environments.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Bacteria are found in all possible habitats wherever life can exist.</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>1 Classification of Kingdom Monera discovered by Dutch naturalist Anton Van Leeuwenhoek in 1675.</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>The branch of biology that deals with the study of bacteria is called bacteriology.</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>Bacteria are the smallest of all known cellular organisms.</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>The average size of bacterial cells ranges from 0.</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>The shape of bacteria and forms of individual bacteria are usually constant while some can change their shape and size with changes in environmental conditions.</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>This ability of organisms is known as pleiomorphism.</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>Detailed notes on 01 monera general introduction structure of bacterial cell mode of nutrition bacterial growth cyanobacteria virus general introduction structure and importance of virus bacteriophage within the introductory microbiology unit.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Bacterial cell: capsule → wall (peptidoglycan) → membrane → cytoplasm (nucleoid, plasmids, 70S ribosomes).</td></tr><tr><td>Gram +ve: thick peptidoglycan (purple); Gram -ve: thin + LPS (pink).</td></tr><tr><td>Shapes: cocci, bacilli, spirilla, spirochaetes; arrangements: diplo-, strepto-, staphylo-.</td></tr><tr><td>Nutrition: autotrophs (photo/chemo) + heterotrophs (saprophytes, parasites).</td></tr><tr><td>Growth curve: lag, log, stationary, death; generation time (E. coli ~20 min).</td></tr><tr><td>Reproduction: binary fission (asexual) + conjugation/transformation/transduction (genetic exchange).</td></tr><tr><td>Endospores: dormant, resistant; survival, not reproduction.</td></tr><tr><td>Cyanobacteria: photosynthetic + nitrogen-fixing (Nostoc, Anabaena).</td></tr><tr><td>Mycoplasma: no wall, smallest living cell; Archaebacteria: extreme environments.</td></tr><tr><td>Bacteria are the simplest, minute, unicellular prokaryotes that can survive even in diverse environments.</td></tr><tr><td>Bacteria are found in all possible habitats wherever life can exist.</td></tr><tr><td>1 Classification of Kingdom Monera discovered by Dutch naturalist Anton Van Leeuwenhoek in 1675.</td></tr><tr><td>The branch of biology that deals with the study of bacteria is called bacteriology.</td></tr><tr><td>Bacteria are the smallest of all known cellular organisms.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Gram-positive = THICK peptidoglycan (retains crystal violet, purple); Gram-negative = THIN peptidoglycan + outer LPS membrane (stains pink with safranin).</td></tr><tr><td>Mesosomes are invaginations of the plasma membrane; they are NOT true organelles (no membrane-bound structure).</td></tr><tr><td>Plasmids are small circular DNA; they are NOT the chromosome (nucleoid).</td></tr><tr><td>Endospores are NOT a mode of reproduction; they are survival structures (one endospore → one bacterium).</td></tr><tr><td>Cyanobacteria are PROKARYOTES (Bacteria domain), NOT true algae; they have no membrane-bound organelles.</td></tr><tr><td>Mycoplasma has NO cell wall; it is the smallest known living cell (0.1–0.3 µm).</td></tr><tr><td>Archaebacteria differ from eubacteria in cell wall (no peptidoglycan) and membrane lipids (ether linkages); they live in extreme environments.</td></tr><tr><td>Binary fission produces two genetically identical daughter cells; conjugation transfers DNA but does NOT increase cell number.</td></tr><tr><td>Distinguish between key concepts in Monera and Bacteria.</td></tr><tr><td>Understand the differences between similar topics.</td></tr><tr><td>Practice identifying the correct application of principles.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Misconception about 01 monera general introduction structure of bacterial cell mode of nutrition bacterial growth cyanobacteria virus general introduction structure and importance of virus bacteriophage. ✅ Scientific explanation.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> A detailed study of Monera reveals the bacterial cell in all its complexity: a capsule, peptidoglycan wall (thick in Gram +ve, thin + LPS in Gram -ve), plasma membrane (with mesosomes in Gram +ve), and cytoplasm containing the naked nucleoid, plasmids, and 70S ribosomes. Bacteria show diverse shapes (cocci, bacilli, spirilla) and arrangements, feed by autotrophy or heterotrophy, and grow through a 4-phase curve (lag, log, stationary, death). They reproduce asexually by binary fission and exchange genes by conjugation, transformation, and transduction. Endospores (Bacillus, Clostridium) are survival structures. Cyanobacteria (Nostoc, Anabaena) photosynthesise and fix nitrogen; Mycoplasma (no wall) is the smallest living cell; archaebacteria thrive in extreme environments. NEB focuses on the detailed bacterial cell, shapes, nutrition, growth, reproduction, cyanobacteria, and special groups. Bacteria  are the simplest,  minute,  unicellular  prokaryotes  that can survive  even  in  diverse
environments.  Bacteria  are  found  in  all  possible  habitats  wherever  life  can  exist.  It  was

