MONERA
Figure 3.1.1 Classification of Kingdom Monera




discovered by Dutch naturalist Anton Van Leeuwenhoek in 1675. The branch of biology that
deals with the study of bacteria is called bacteriology.
Bacteria are the smallest  of all known cellular  organisms. Th Summary of 01 monera general introduction structure of bacterial cell mode of nutrition bacterial growth cyanobacteria virus general introduction structure and importance of virus bacteriophage.</p><hr><h2>Monera: General introduction, structure of bacterial cell…</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Scope.** Monera: General introduction, structure of bacterial cell… — Introductory Microbiology, Biology (class-11-notes).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Tested.** define it · list: monera, structure of bacterial cell, mode of nutrition, bacterial growth · one worked example · one misconception each.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Structure of bacterial cell.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Mode of nutrition.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Bacterial growth.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Cyanobacteria.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Blue green algae.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Monera</td></tr><tr><td>• Structure of bacterial cell</td></tr><tr><td>• Mode of nutrition</td></tr><tr><td>• Bacterial growth</td></tr><tr><td>• Cyanobacteria</td></tr><tr><td>• Blue green algae</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat "Monera" as one blob → ✅ split into: monera, structure of bacterial cell, mode of nutrition.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ no diagram → ✅ add one labelled figure / structured list.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Monera: General introduction, structure of bacterial cell… (Introductory Microbiology, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Monera, structure of bacterial cell, mode of nutrition. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Impacts of Biotechnology in Microbiology</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>DETAILED RECOMBINANT DNA (rDNA) TECHNOLOGY AND ITS APPLICATIONS.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>rDNA (gene cloning) — 7 steps:</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Step 1 — Identification + isolation: the gene of interest (e.g., human insulin) is identified and isolated from the source DNA (or synthesised by PCR).</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Step 2 — Cutting: restriction endonucleases (e.g., EcoRI, BamHI) cut both the source DNA and the vector (plasmid) at specific recognition sites, producing sticky (or blunt) ends.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Step 3 — Insertion (ligation): DNA ligase joins the gene into the vector (plasmid), forming recombinant DNA (rDNA).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Step 4 — Transformation: the rDNA is introduced into a competent host cell (E. coli) by heat shock, electroporation, or a calcium-chloride method.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Step 5 — Selection: the host is grown on a selective medium (e.g., ampicillin); only cells with the plasmid (carrying the antibiotic-resistance gene) survive; blue-white screening (lacZ) identifies recombinants.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Step 6 — Expression: the host cell transcribes + translates the inserted gene, producing the desired protein (e.g., insulin).</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Step 7 — Harvest + purification: the protein is secreted into the culture medium (or inside the cell), harvested, purified (chromatography), and formulated.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Vectors: plasmids (most common), bacteriophages, cosmids, BACs, YACs; features: origin of replication (ori), selectable marker (antibiotic resistance), multiple cloning site (MCS), and a high copy number.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Host cells: E. coli (prokaryotic, fast, cheap), yeast (eukaryotic, for proteins needing glycosylation), mammalian cells (for complex human proteins).</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Applications in medicine: recombinant insulin (E. coli, 1982), growth hormone, hepatitis B + HPV vaccines, monoclonal antibodies, gene therapy.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Applications in agriculture: Bt cotton (Cry toxin gene → insect resistance), herbicide-tolerant soybean, tissue culture (rapid propagation), biofertilisers (Rhizobium, Azotobacter), biopesticides.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Applications in industry: enzyme production (amylase, protease in detergents), bioremediation (oil degradation by Pseudomonas), biofuels (ethanol by yeast), biogas (methanogens).</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Gene therapy: a functional copy of a defective gene is delivered to the patient's cells via a viral vector (e.g., adeno-associated virus); examples: SCID (1990), cystic fibrosis (trials).</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Gene editing (CRISPR-Cas9): a programmable nuclease cuts DNA at a specific site; the cell's repair machinery introduces the desired change; derived from the bacterial immune system (2012 Nobel).</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Ethical considerations: GMO food safety, gene patenting, genetic privacy, environmental release of transgenics, and the creation of "designer babies."</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>NEB focus: detailed rDNA steps, vectors, host cells, insulin production, gene therapy, CRISPR, and the ethical issues of biotechnology.</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>The use of microbial biotechnology is described below: 1.</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>In Medicine: The concept of microbial biotechnology is used in human and veterinary medicine by the following ways: a.</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>Antibiotics: Antibiotics are substances obtained from microorganisms that help in controlling different types of diseases.</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>They are obtained from different types of microorganisms.</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>Vaccines: Vaccines are the substances derived from microorganisms used to immunize against disease.</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td>Vaccines are produced by using the concept of fermentation technology and recombinant DNA technology.</td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td>The use of vaccines such as smallpox, polio, tetanus etc.</td></tr><tr><td>26</td><td>led to the virtual elimination of these diseases in regions of the world where the vaccines have been deployed.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>rDNA: 7 steps (isolate, cut, ligate, transform, select, express, harvest).</td></tr><tr><td>Vectors: plasmids, phages, cosmids, BACs, YACs; features: ori, selectable marker, MCS.</td></tr><tr><td>Host cells: E. coli (prokaryotic), yeast (eukaryotic), mammalian cells.</td></tr><tr><td>Medical: insulin (1982), vaccines (HBV, HPV), monoclonal antibodies, gene therapy.</td></tr><tr><td>Agricultural: Bt crops, herbicide-tolerant crops, tissue culture, biofertilisers.</td></tr><tr><td>Industrial: enzymes, bioremediation, biofuels, biogas.</td></tr><tr><td>Gene therapy (correct a faulty gene) vs gene editing (CRISPR tool).</td></tr><tr><td>Ethical: GMO safety, patenting, privacy, environment, designer babies.</td></tr><tr><td>• Impacts of biotechnology in the field of microbiology</td></tr><tr><td>The use of microbial biotechnology is described below: 1.</td></tr><tr><td>In Medicine: The concept of microbial biotechnology is used in human and veterinary medicine by the following ways: a.</td></tr><tr><td>Antibiotics: Antibiotics are substances obtained from microorganisms that help in controlling different types of diseases.</td></tr><tr><td>They are obtained from different types of microorganisms.</td></tr><tr><td>Vaccines: Vaccines are the substances derived from microorganisms used to immunize against disease.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Restriction enzymes CUT DNA at specific sequences; DNA ligase JOINS DNA fragments; they are different tools.</td></tr><tr><td>A plasmid is a small circular DNA molecule used as a VECTOR; it is NOT the gene of interest itself.</td></tr><tr><td>Transformation (in rDNA) = introducing foreign DNA into a competent cell; it is DIFFERENT from transformation (natural DNA uptake in bacteria).</td></tr><tr><td>Gene therapy treats a disease by CORRECTING a faulty gene in the patient's cells; gene editing (CRISPR) is a TOOL to make that correction.</td></tr><tr><td>Bt toxin (from Bacillus thuringiensis) is harmless to humans but lethal to specific insect larvae; it is used in Bt crops.</td></tr><tr><td>Monoclonal antibodies are produced by a single B-cell clone; they are highly specific and used in diagnostics + therapy.</td></tr><tr><td>CRISPR-Cas9 is a programmable nuclease; it is NOT the same as a restriction enzyme (which cuts at a fixed, natural sequence).</td></tr><tr><td>A transgenic organism (GMO) carries a gene from a DIFFERENT species; it is created in the laboratory.</td></tr><tr><td>Distinguish between key concepts in Biotechnology in Microbiology.</td></tr><tr><td>Understand the differences between similar topics.</td></tr><tr><td>Practice identifying the correct application of principles.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Recombinant DNA (rDNA) technology follows 7 detailed steps: isolate the gene, cut it with restriction enzymes, ligate it into a vector, transform a host cell, select the transformants, express the gene, and harvest the product. Plasmids are the most common vectors (ori, selectable marker, MCS). E. coli is the standard host for simple proteins; yeast and mammalian cells are used for complex, glycosylated proteins. Applications span medicine (insulin, vaccines, gene therapy), agriculture (Bt crops, tissue culture, biofertilisers), and industry (enzymes, bioremediation, biofuels). Gene therapy corrects faulty genes in the patient's cells; gene editing (CRISPR-Cas9) is the tool that makes the correction. Ethical issues (GMO safety, patenting, privacy, environment) are integral to the field. NEB focuses on the detailed rDNA steps, vectors, host cells, insulin production, gene therapy, CRISPR, and the ethical issues of biotechnology. Impacts of biotechnology in the field of microbiology (Introductory Microbiology, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Impacts of biotechnology in the field of microbiology. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these. The use of microbial biotechnology is described below:
1. In Medicine:
The concept  of  microbial  biotechnology  is  used  in human  and  veterinary  medicine  by the
following ways:
a. Antibiotics:
Antibiotics  are  substances obtained  from  microorganisms   that  help  in  controlling
different types of diseases. They are obtained from different types of microorganisms.
b. Vaccines:
Vaccines  are  the substances derived from  microorganisms  used to immunize  against
disease.</p><hr><h2>Virus: General introduction, structure and importance of …</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Viruses: acellular, obligate intracellular parasites; not classified in any kingdom; consist of a nucleic acid core (DNA or RNA) + protein coat (capsid); some have a lipid envelope.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Structure: capsomeres (protein subunits of capsid); bacteriophages have a head (icosahedral) + tail; animal viruses may have an envelope with glycoprotein spikes.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Types by nucleic acid: DNA viruses (e.g., T4 phage, smallpox) and RNA viruses (e.g., influenza, HIV, SARS-CoV-2).</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Bacteriophage (T4): lytic life cycle — adsorption → injection → replication → assembly → release (lysogeny is the lysogenic cycle in lambda phage).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Replication: viruses hijack the host cell machinery; they cannot replicate independently.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Importance: many human and animal diseases (common cold, flu, HIV/AIDS, hepatitis, rabies, smallpox — eradicated in 1980).</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Applications: viral vectors in gene therapy, bacteriophages in food safety testing, viruses in molecular biology tools.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>NEB focus: virus structure, lytic cycle of T4 phage, and importance of viruses.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Virus</td></tr><tr><td>• Structure</td></tr><tr><td>• Importance of virus</td></tr><tr><td>• Bacteriophage</td></tr><tr><td>Viruses are acellular, obligate intracellular parasites.</td></tr><tr><td>Structure: nucleic acid + capsid (+ envelope in some).</td></tr><tr><td>DNA or RNA (not both); capsomeres form the capsid.</td></tr><tr><td>Bacteriophage: icosahedral head + tail + baseplate + tail fibers.</td></tr><tr><td>Lytic cycle: 5 steps, destroys host; Lysogenic: integrates, delays lysis.</td></tr><tr><td>Not living: no metabolism, no independent reproduction.</td></tr><tr><td>Importance: cause many diseases; used in gene therapy and research.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Viruses are NOT living organisms: they lack metabolism, cannot reproduce outside a host, and are not made of cells.</td></tr><tr><td>A virus has EITHER DNA or RNA, never both (with rare exceptions like some retroviruses that convert RNA to DNA).</td></tr><tr><td>Bacteriophages infect BACTERIA; animal viruses infect animals; plant viruses infect plants.</td></tr><tr><td>The capsid is protein; the core is nucleic acid; the envelope (if present) is lipid + glycoproteins from the host membrane.</td></tr><tr><td>Lytic cycle destroys the host cell; lysogenic cycle integrates viral DNA into the host genome (prophage) without immediate lysis.</td></tr><tr><td>Viruses are NOT visible under a light microscope; they require an electron microscope (~20–300 nm size).</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Viruses are acellular, obligate intracellular parasites that contain either DNA or RNA enclosed in a protein capsid (and sometimes a lipid envelope). They cannot replicate outside a host cell and hijack host machinery. The T4 bacteriophage lytic cycle (adsorption, injection, replication, assembly, release) is the NEB model. Viruses cause many important diseases but are also valuable tools in gene therapy and molecular biology. NEB examinations focus on virus structure, the lytic cycle, and the distinction between viruses and living organisms.</p><hr><h2>Virus</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Viruses: acellular, obligate intracellular parasites; not classified in any kingdom; consist of a nucleic acid core (DNA or RNA) + protein coat (capsid); some have a lipid envelope.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Structure: capsomeres (protein subunits of capsid); bacteriophages have a head (icosahedral) + tail; animal viruses may have an envelope with glycoprotein spikes.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Types by nucleic acid: DNA viruses (e.g., T4 phage, smallpox) and RNA viruses (e.g., influenza, HIV, SARS-CoV-2).</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Bacteriophage (T4): lytic life cycle — adsorption → injection → replication → assembly → release (lysogeny is the lysogenic cycle in lambda phage).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Replication: viruses hijack the host cell machinery; they cannot replicate independently.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Importance: many human and animal diseases (common cold, flu, HIV/AIDS, hepatitis, rabies, smallpox — eradicated in 1980).</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Applications: viral vectors in gene therapy, bacteriophages in food safety testing, viruses in molecular biology tools.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>NEB focus: virus structure, lytic cycle of T4 phage, and importance of viruses.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Viruses are acellular, obligate intracellular parasites.</td></tr><tr><td>Structure: nucleic acid + capsid (+ envelope in some).</td></tr><tr><td>DNA or RNA (not both); capsomeres form the capsid.</td></tr><tr><td>Bacteriophage: icosahedral head + tail + baseplate + tail fibers.</td></tr><tr><td>Lytic cycle: 5 steps, destroys host; Lysogenic: integrates, delays lysis.</td></tr><tr><td>Not living: no metabolism, no independent reproduction.</td></tr><tr><td>Importance: cause many diseases; used in gene therapy and research.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Viruses are NOT living organisms: they lack metabolism, cannot reproduce outside a host, and are not made of cells.</td></tr><tr><td>A virus has EITHER DNA or RNA, never both (with rare exceptions like some retroviruses that convert RNA to DNA).</td></tr><tr><td>Bacteriophages infect BACTERIA; animal viruses infect animals; plant viruses infect plants.</td></tr><tr><td>The capsid is protein; the core is nucleic acid; the envelope (if present) is lipid + glycoproteins from the host membrane.</td></tr><tr><td>Lytic cycle destroys the host cell; lysogenic cycle integrates viral DNA into the host genome (prophage) without immediate lysis.</td></tr><tr><td>Viruses are NOT visible under a light microscope; they require an electron microscope (~20–300 nm size).</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Viruses are acellular, obligate intracellular parasites that contain either DNA or RNA enclosed in a protein capsid (and sometimes a lipid envelope). They cannot replicate outside a host cell and hijack host machinery. The T4 bacteriophage lytic cycle (adsorption, injection, replication, assembly, release) is the NEB model. Viruses cause many important diseases but are also valuable tools in gene therapy and molecular biology. NEB examinations focus on virus structure, the lytic cycle, and the distinction between viruses and living organisms.</p><hr><h2>Biotechnology in Microbiology</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>DETAILED RECOMBINANT DNA (rDNA) TECHNOLOGY AND ITS APPLICATIONS.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>rDNA (gene cloning) — 7 steps:</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Step 1 — Identification + isolation: the gene of interest (e.g., human insulin) is identified and isolated from the source DNA (or synthesised by PCR).</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Step 2 — Cutting: restriction endonucleases (e.g., EcoRI, BamHI) cut both the source DNA and the vector (plasmid) at specific recognition sites, producing sticky (or blunt) ends.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Step 3 — Insertion (ligation): DNA ligase joins the gene into the vector (plasmid), forming recombinant DNA (rDNA).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Step 4 — Transformation: the rDNA is introduced into a competent host cell (E. coli) by heat shock, electroporation, or a calcium-chloride method.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Step 5 — Selection: the host is grown on a selective medium (e.g., ampicillin); only cells with the plasmid (carrying the antibiotic-resistance gene) survive; blue-white screening (lacZ) identifies recombinants.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Step 6 — Expression: the host cell transcribes + translates the inserted gene, producing the desired protein (e.g., insulin).</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Step 7 — Harvest + purification: the protein is secreted into the culture medium (or inside the cell), harvested, purified (chromatography), and formulated.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Vectors: plasmids (most common), bacteriophages, cosmids, BACs, YACs; features: origin of replication (ori), selectable marker (antibiotic resistance), multiple cloning site (MCS), and a high copy number.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Host cells: E. coli (prokaryotic, fast, cheap), yeast (eukaryotic, for proteins needing glycosylation), mammalian cells (for complex human proteins).</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Applications in medicine: recombinant insulin (E. coli, 1982), growth hormone, hepatitis B + HPV vaccines, monoclonal antibodies, gene therapy.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Applications in agriculture: Bt cotton (Cry toxin gene → insect resistance), herbicide-tolerant soybean, tissue culture (rapid propagation), biofertilisers (Rhizobium, Azotobacter), biopesticides.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Applications in industry: enzyme production (amylase, protease in detergents), bioremediation (oil degradation by Pseudomonas), biofuels (ethanol by yeast), biogas (methanogens).</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Gene therapy: a functional copy of a defective gene is delivered to the patient's cells via a viral vector (e.g., adeno-associated virus); examples: SCID (1990), cystic fibrosis (trials).</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Gene editing (CRISPR-Cas9): a programmable nuclease cuts DNA at a specific site; the cell's repair machinery introduces the desired change; derived from the bacterial immune system (2012 Nobel).</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Ethical considerations: GMO food safety, gene patenting, genetic privacy, environmental release of transgenics, and the creation of "designer babies."</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>NEB focus: detailed rDNA steps, vectors, host cells, insulin production, gene therapy, CRISPR, and the ethical issues of biotechnology.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>rDNA: 7 steps (isolate, cut, ligate, transform, select, express, harvest).</td></tr><tr><td>Vectors: plasmids, phages, cosmids, BACs, YACs; features: ori, selectable marker, MCS.</td></tr><tr><td>Host cells: E. coli (prokaryotic), yeast (eukaryotic), mammalian cells.</td></tr><tr><td>Medical: insulin (1982), vaccines (HBV, HPV), monoclonal antibodies, gene therapy.</td></tr><tr><td>Agricultural: Bt crops, herbicide-tolerant crops, tissue culture, biofertilisers.</td></tr><tr><td>Industrial: enzymes, bioremediation, biofuels, biogas.</td></tr><tr><td>Gene therapy (correct a faulty gene) vs gene editing (CRISPR tool).</td></tr><tr><td>Ethical: GMO safety, patenting, privacy, environment, designer babies.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Restriction enzymes CUT DNA at specific sequences; DNA ligase JOINS DNA fragments; they are different tools.</td></tr><tr><td>A plasmid is a small circular DNA molecule used as a VECTOR; it is NOT the gene of interest itself.</td></tr><tr><td>Transformation (in rDNA) = introducing foreign DNA into a competent cell; it is DIFFERENT from transformation (natural DNA uptake in bacteria).</td></tr><tr><td>Gene therapy treats a disease by CORRECTING a faulty gene in the patient's cells; gene editing (CRISPR) is a TOOL to make that correction.</td></tr><tr><td>Bt toxin (from Bacillus thuringiensis) is harmless to humans but lethal to specific insect larvae; it is used in Bt crops.</td></tr><tr><td>Monoclonal antibodies are produced by a single B-cell clone; they are highly specific and used in diagnostics + therapy.</td></tr><tr><td>CRISPR-Cas9 is a programmable nuclease; it is NOT the same as a restriction enzyme (which cuts at a fixed, natural sequence).</td></tr><tr><td>A transgenic organism (GMO) carries a gene from a DIFFERENT species; it is created in the laboratory.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Recombinant DNA (rDNA) technology follows 7 detailed steps: isolate the gene, cut it with restriction enzymes, ligate it into a vector, transform a host cell, select the transformants, express the gene, and harvest the product. Plasmids are the most common vectors (ori, selectable marker, MCS). E. coli is the standard host for simple proteins; yeast and mammalian cells are used for complex, glycosylated proteins. Applications span medicine (insulin, vaccines, gene therapy), agriculture (Bt crops, tissue culture, biofertilisers), and industry (enzymes, bioremediation, biofuels). Gene therapy corrects faulty genes in the patient's cells; gene editing (CRISPR-Cas9) is the tool that makes the correction. Ethical issues (GMO safety, patenting, privacy, environment) are integral to the field. NEB focuses on the detailed rDNA steps, vectors, host cells, insulin production, gene therapy, CRISPR, and the ethical issues of biotechnology.</p><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Monera and Bacteria</td><td>Kingdom Monera comprises prokaryotic organisms: bacteria, cyanobacteria, mycoplasma, and archaebacteria. Bacteria show diverse shapes, nutrition, and reproduction (binary fission + genetic exchange). The growth curve (lag, log, stationary, death) and Gram staining are fundamental microbiology tools. Cyanobacteria fix nitrogen and photosynthesise. Bacteria are ecologically indispensable as decomposers, nitrogen fixers, and symbionts, and economically vital in food, industry, and medicine.</td></tr><tr><td>2: Introductory Microbiology — Bacteria and Viruses</td><td>A detailed study of Monera reveals the bacterial cell in all its complexity: a capsule, peptidoglycan wall (thick in Gram +ve, thin + LPS in Gram -ve), plasma membrane (with mesosomes in Gram +ve), and cytoplasm containing the naked nucleoid, plasmids, and 70S ribosomes. Bacteria show diverse shapes (cocci, bacilli, spirilla) and arrangements, feed by autotrophy or heterotrophy, and grow through a 4-phase curve (lag, log, stationary, death). They reproduce asexually by binary fission and exchange genes by conjugation, transformation, and transduction. Endospores (Bacillus, Clostridium) are survival structures. Cyanobacteria (Nostoc, Anabaena) photosynthesise and fix nitrogen; Mycoplasma (no wall) is the smallest living cell; archaebacteria thrive in extreme environments. NEB focuses on the detailed bacterial cell, shapes, nutrition, growth, reproduction, cyanobacteria, and special groups. Bacteria  are the simplest,  minute,  unicellular  prokaryotes  that can survive  even  in  diverse
environments.  Bacteria  are  found  in  all  possible  habitats  wherever  life  can  exist.  It  was

