discovered by Dutch naturalist Anton Van Leeuwenhoek in 1675. The branch of biology that
deals with the study of bacteria is called bacteriology.
Bacteria are the smallest  of all known cellular  organisms. Th Summary of 01 monera general introduction structure of bacterial cell mode of nutrition bacterial growth cyanobacteria virus general introduction structure and importance of virus bacteriophage.</td></tr><tr><td>3: Monera: General introduction, structure of bacterial cell…</td><td>Monera: General introduction, structure of bacterial cell… (Introductory Microbiology, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Monera, structure of bacterial cell, mode of nutrition. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>4: Impacts of Biotechnology in Microbiology</td><td>Recombinant DNA (rDNA) technology follows 7 detailed steps: isolate the gene, cut it with restriction enzymes, ligate it into a vector, transform a host cell, select the transformants, express the gene, and harvest the product. Plasmids are the most common vectors (ori, selectable marker, MCS). E. coli is the standard host for simple proteins; yeast and mammalian cells are used for complex, glycosylated proteins. Applications span medicine (insulin, vaccines, gene therapy), agriculture (Bt crops, tissue culture, biofertilisers), and industry (enzymes, bioremediation, biofuels). Gene therapy corrects faulty genes in the patient's cells; gene editing (CRISPR-Cas9) is the tool that makes the correction. Ethical issues (GMO safety, patenting, privacy, environment) are integral to the field. NEB focuses on the detailed rDNA steps, vectors, host cells, insulin production, gene therapy, CRISPR, and the ethical issues of biotechnology. Impacts of biotechnology in the field of microbiology (Introductory Microbiology, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Impacts of biotechnology in the field of microbiology. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these. The use of microbial biotechnology is described below:
1. In Medicine:
The concept  of  microbial  biotechnology  is  used  in human  and  veterinary  medicine  by the
following ways:
a. Antibiotics:
Antibiotics  are  substances obtained  from  microorganisms   that  help  in  controlling
different types of diseases. They are obtained from different types of microorganisms.
b. Vaccines:
Vaccines  are  the substances derived from  microorganisms  used to immunize  against
disease.</td></tr><tr><td>5: Virus: General introduction, structure and importance of …</td><td>Viruses are acellular, obligate intracellular parasites that contain either DNA or RNA enclosed in a protein capsid (and sometimes a lipid envelope). They cannot replicate outside a host cell and hijack host machinery. The T4 bacteriophage lytic cycle (adsorption, injection, replication, assembly, release) is the NEB model. Viruses cause many important diseases but are also valuable tools in gene therapy and molecular biology. NEB examinations focus on virus structure, the lytic cycle, and the distinction between viruses and living organisms.</td></tr><tr><td>6: Virus</td><td>Viruses are acellular, obligate intracellular parasites that contain either DNA or RNA enclosed in a protein capsid (and sometimes a lipid envelope). They cannot replicate outside a host cell and hijack host machinery. The T4 bacteriophage lytic cycle (adsorption, injection, replication, assembly, release) is the NEB model. Viruses cause many important diseases but are also valuable tools in gene therapy and molecular biology. NEB examinations focus on virus structure, the lytic cycle, and the distinction between viruses and living organisms.</td></tr><tr><td>7: Biotechnology in Microbiology</td><td>Recombinant DNA (rDNA) technology follows 7 detailed steps: isolate the gene, cut it with restriction enzymes, ligate it into a vector, transform a host cell, select the transformants, express the gene, and harvest the product. Plasmids are the most common vectors (ori, selectable marker, MCS). E. coli is the standard host for simple proteins; yeast and mammalian cells are used for complex, glycosylated proteins. Applications span medicine (insulin, vaccines, gene therapy), agriculture (Bt crops, tissue culture, biofertilisers), and industry (enzymes, bioremediation, biofuels). Gene therapy corrects faulty genes in the patient's cells; gene editing (CRISPR-Cas9) is the tool that makes the correction. Ethical issues (GMO safety, patenting, privacy, environment) are integral to the field. NEB focuses on the detailed rDNA steps, vectors, host cells, insulin production, gene therapy, CRISPR, and the ethical issues of biotechnology.</td></tr></table></body></html>